## [Chunk 1] Import data, descriptive stats, create subsets
Here we are loading the 3 packages you'll need for graphing (dplyr for summarizing data, ggplot2 for plotting, viridis for colorblind friendly palettes) and installing/loading the data you will need for this plotting exercise.

If you haven't installed these packages before, install them from the "Packages" tab.
```{r}
#loading in package for graphing
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)
library(viridis)

#Installing and loading package with data that was made available by the Palmer Station, Antarctica LTER (Long Term Ecological Research Network)
library(palmerpenguins)

#Summary table of the loaded data
summary(penguins)

```


## [Chunk 2] Summarizing data for plotting means in a bar chart
To make barplots in base and ggplot you need to first make a summary table of the means and standard error that you will be plotting. This code groups the data by a categorical variable and then calculates averages and standard deviations across those groups.

This function uses the library dplyr which is a package used to manipulate dataframes. The functions we will use are similar to the function tapply, which we have used before, but puts everything into neat, usable dataframe. Don't worry too much about how it works, it will get your the mean and standard deviation of your categories: 

```{r}
#Mean and sd bill length by species
penguin_summary_sp <- penguins%>%
  group_by(species)%>% #grouping data by species
  summarize(mean_bill_length= mean(bill_length_mm,na.rm=TRUE),
            sd_bill_length= sd(bill_length_mm,na.rm=TRUE)) #calculate mean and st dev of bill length

#Mean and sd bill length by species & sex
penguin_summary_sp_sex <- penguins%>%
    filter(sex!="NA")%>% #drops the NAs
  group_by(species, sex)%>% #group by species and sex
  summarize(mean_bill_length= mean(bill_length_mm,na.rm=TRUE),
            sd_bill_length= sd(bill_length_mm,na.rm=TRUE))
```



# Bar plots
## Bar plots in base R
First, we will plot this the same way we have all semester. We will plot average bill lengths by species of penguin. 

### [Chunk 3] Penguin bill length by species (base R)
```{r}
#No need to modify this code, just run and look at the plot
SpeciesBars<-barplot(mean_bill_length~species, data=penguin_summary_sp, 
                     las=2, #rotate axis labels
                     col = "dodgerblue", #set bar color
                     border = "black", #set border color
                     ylim = c(0, 60), #set y-axis limits
                     ylab="Bill length (mm)", #write y-axis label
                     xlab= "") #no x-axis label

#Now if we want to add standard error bars, this is what we would need to do
with(penguin_summary_sp, #to save keystrokes, set the dataframe name
     arrows(x0=SpeciesBars,y0=mean_bill_length-sd_bill_length, #set starting coordinate for error bar
            y1=mean_bill_length+sd_bill_length, #set end coordinate for error bar
            lwd = 1.5, #set line thickness
            code = 3, #arrow head on both sides of line
            angle = 90, #change from arrow to flat bar
            length = 0.05)) #set length of whisker
```

### [Chunk 4] Penguin bill length by species and sex (base R)
If we wanted to make a grouped bar plot where we show average bill length broken down by sex and species, it would look something like this.

```{r }

barcol <- viridis(3)[penguin_summary_sp_sex$species]
  #Viridis is a color-blind friendly palette
  #you control the number of colors generated in parenthesis
  #makes a list of colors to use for the 6 bars

barplot(penguin_summary_sp_sex$mean_bill_length, 
        col=barcol, border="grey70", #adjust colors
        names.arg=penguin_summary_sp_sex$sex, #write bar labels
        las=2, #rotate axis labels 
        xlab="",ylab="Bill length (mm)", #write axis labels
        ylim=c(0,60)) #set axis limits

legend(x=0,y=70, #x and y location of legend
       levels(penguins$species), #labels for legend
       fill=viridis(3), #colors for legend
       border="grey70", #color of border
       horiz=T, #make legend horizontal
       bty="n", #no box around the legend
       xpd = T) #expand the plotting area to allow legend in margin

box() #add a box around the plotting area

```



## Bar plots in ggplot
ggplot is a package designed for the purpose of making visual graphics. Many R users make graphs with the ggplot package.

### [Chunk 5] Penguin bill length by species (ggplot)

```{r, echo=TRUE}

#Here we are again plotting average bill length by species, but note the differences in syntax
ggplot(penguin_summary_sp, aes(x=species, y=mean_bill_length))+ #identifies data and variables to plot
  geom_bar(stat="identity", #identifies what type of plot element you want to add (bars, points) 
           fill="steelblue4",   #set bar colors
            width=0.5) +  #allows you to control the width of the bars
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin=mean_bill_length-sd_bill_length,  #set start coordinate for error bar (mean - sd)
                ymax=mean_bill_length+sd_bill_length),  #set start coordinate for error bar (mean + sd)
                width=.1)+ #controls the width of error bar
  xlab("Penguin Species")+ #x axis title
  ylab("Average bill length (mm)")+ #y axis title
  theme_classic() #adjusts the background elements


```

### [Chunk 6] Penguin bill length by species and sex (ggplot)
We can also do plot species and sex in a single graph.

```{r, echo=TRUE}

ggplot(penguin_summary_sp_sex, aes(x=species, y=mean_bill_length, fill = sex))+ #sets the variables and dataset, specifying fill here groups the data by sex
  geom_bar(stat="identity", color="black",position=position_dodge(),width=0.5)+  #identifies what type of plot element you want to add (bars)
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin=mean_bill_length-sd_bill_length, ymax=mean_bill_length+sd_bill_length), width=.1,position=position_dodge(.5))+ #setting coordinates for error bars
  xlab("Penguin Species")+ #Naming the x-axis title
  ylab("Bill Length (mm)")+ #Naming the y-axis title
  theme_classic()+ #sets a clean background style for the figure
  scale_fill_manual(values=c("olivedrab4", "lightsteelblue2")) #choosing our color scheme. There are also other ways to use preset color packages like (scale_color_brewer(), viridis, or the package NatParksPalettes)

```

