# Scatterplots

## [Chunk 8] Scatter plots using base R graphics
First, this is how you would plot the relationship of flipper length and body mass using base R, which we have used up to this point in lab

```{r, echo=TRUE}
 #It's better to create an object holding color names if you are going to use them in multiple plot elements
sp_col <- c("steelblue1", "darkgreen", "goldenrod")

plot(flipper_length_mm~body_mass_g, data=penguins, 
     las=2, #rotate axis numbers
     col=sp_col[species], #set colors by species
     pch=c(16,2,4)[species], #symbols by species
     ylab="Flipper length (mm)", xlab= "Body mass (mm)") #set axis labels

#Need to run the ANCOVA to get the values for the slope
flipmod <- aov(flipper_length_mm~body_mass_g*species,data=penguins)

#Plotting best-fit lines for an ANCOVA can be complex
for(i in 1:3){
  sp <- levels(penguins$species)[i]
  maxl <- max(penguins[penguins$species==sp,'body_mass_g'],na.rm=T)
  minl <- min(penguins[penguins$species==sp,'body_mass_g'],na.rm=T)
  pts <- predict(flipmod,newdata = data.frame(species=rep(sp,2),body_mass_g=c(minl,maxl)))
  points(pts~c(minl,maxl),type="l",col=sp_col[i],lwd=2)
  print(pts)
}

legend("topleft", #location
       levels(penguins$species), #labels
       bty="n", #no box
       col=sp_col, #colors
       pch=c(16,2,4), #symbol types
       lwd=2) #line width

```


## [Chunk 9] Scatter plots using ggplot

This is how we would plot the same thing (flipper length v body mass) but in ggplot
```{r, echo=TRUE}
#we can also set color palettes to use in ggplot figures
sp_col_2 <- c("slateblue3","maroon", "slategrey")

ggplot(aes(y=flipper_length_mm, x=body_mass_g, color=species), data=penguins)+ #identifies data and variables, must set grouping factor in this line of code (either through color= grouping variable or shape= grouping variable)
  geom_point(size=1.25, na.rm=TRUE)+ #sets size of points and removes points that have NAs
    theme_classic()+ #sets background theme
  geom_smooth(method="lm", se=FALSE, na.rm=TRUE)+ #adds a regression lime for each species (based off variables in aes)
    scale_color_manual(values=sp_col_2, name= "Species")+ #setting color values to those listed in color palette and setting legend title. Note that fill is used for bars, and color is used for points
    xlab("Body mass (g)")+ #name x-axis title
   ylab("Flipper length (mm)")+ #name y-axis title
  theme(legend.position = c(0.2, 0.8)) #legend location via coordinates
          #ylim(150, 240) #use this code to adjust the y axis range

```

